<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=polyethylene">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polyethylene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as polythene, polyethene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Polyethylene is a polymer of ethylene and the most commonly produced plastic, used mainly for packaging. The German chemist Hans von Pechmann first prepared it by accident in 1898 while investigating diazomethane; his colleagues Eugen Bamberger and Friedrich Tschirner found long −CH2− chains in the white, waxy substance and called it polymethylene. The first industrially practical synthesis was again an accident, in 1933 at the Imperial Chemical Industries works in Northwich, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>; Michael Perrin made it reproducible in 1935, and low-density production began in 1939. Catalysts discovered in 1951 and 1953 made high-density polyethylene possible at mild temperatures and pressures.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polyethylene.jpg" alt="Polyethylene" width="330" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lluis tgn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polyethylene_balls1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
